## How Cursor Becomes an Agentic Coder

### The Extension Synergy

Cursor's 18 extensions work together to create a full agentic coding experience through a layered pipeline:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as User
    participant S as cursor-socket<br/>(TCP transport)
    participant W as cursor-agent-worker<br/>(agent launcher)
    participant E as cursor-agent-exec<br/>(AI executor)
    participant R as cursor-retrieval<br/>(code search)
    participant F as cursor-file-service<br/>(file ops)
    participant C as cursor-checkout<br/>(git ops)
    participant A as Cursor Cloud<br/>(backend API)
    
    U->>W: Type prompt in Composer
    W->>E: Spawn agent process
    E->>A: Send prompt to AI model
    A->>E: Return tool call request
    
    alt "Read code"
        E->>R: search / grep / semantic
        R->>E: Return matching code
    else "Write code"
        E->>F: read / write / edit
        F->>E: File operations complete
    else "Run command"
        E->>E: Execute via agent store
        E->>E: better-sqlite3 for state
    else "Git operation"
        E->>C: checkout / commit
        C->>E: Git result
    end
    
    E->>A: Send tool result to model
    A->>E: Final response
    E->>S: Return via socket
    S->>U: Display in Composer
```

### Agent Workflow Through Extensions

```
1. User Input (Composer UI)
   → cursor-socket receives message
   → cursor-agent-worker processes
   → cursor-agent-exec executes

2. Context Loading (AI Needs Code Context)
   → cursor-resolver loads settings
   → cursor-retrieval finds relevant files
      → Grep (text patterns via ripgrep)
      → Glob (file patterns)
      → SemanticSearch (vector embeddings)
   → Rules and skills loaded via agent-worker

3. Agent Processing (AI Decides Tools)
   → cursor-agent-worker runs agent loop
   → Tool selection based on task type

4. Tool Execution (Running the Tools)
   → cursor-file-service for file reads/writes
   → cursor-agent-exec for shell commands
   → cursor-retrieval for searches (Grep/Glob/SemanticSearch)
   → cursor-mcp + cursor-browser-automation for MCP tools
   → cursor-checkout for Git branch operations

5. Result Integration
   → Results fed back to agent-exec
   → Agent evaluates and continues loop

6. User Feedback (Displayed in Editor)
   → Results sent via cursor-socket
   → Displayed in Composer UI
   → File changes shown in explorer
   → Git operations reflected in SCM view
```

### Native Dependencies: Why Cursor Needs Rust

Cursor uses 3 native extensions compiled to Node.js add-ons:

| Native Extension | Purpose | Used By |
|-----------------|---------|---------|
| `better-sqlite3` | Fast, synchronous SQLite for agent state persistence | `cursor-agent-exec` |
| `tree-sitter` | C-based incremental parser for code AST analysis | `cursor-agent-exec` |
| `@anysphere/tree-chunk-napi` | Rust-based custom code chunking/tree analysis | `cursor-agent-exec` |

These provide the raw performance needed for:
- Parsing source code ASTs in real-time during agent execution
- Chunking code into context-window-sized pieces for AI prompts
- Persisting agent state atomically via SQLite

